# Building a Cadencr custom tab

You are working in a **custom tab**: a static web app that Cadencr shows as a
tab on a conversation (feature) page, next to Agent, Terminal, Git, Editor and
Browser. This folder is the whole tab. Do not modify Cadencr itself.

## How it runs

- Cadencr loads `dist/index.html` (the manifest's `entry`) in an isolated view
  at `cadencr-tab://<id>/`. Build output must use relative URLs (`base: "./"`
  in `vite.config.ts` — keep it).
- The page has **no network access** (`connect-src 'none'`). `fetch`,
  WebSockets, remote images, fonts and scripts from the internet all fail.
  Everything goes through the SDK, which talks to Cadencr over a private
  channel. Bundle every asset you need.
- No inline `<script>`, no `eval`, no iframes, no forms that submit, no
  `window.open`. Inline styles are allowed.
- Cadencr watches `tab.json` and `dist/`. Run `npm run dev` (`vite build
  --watch`) and every rebuild reloads the open tab.

## Commands

```sh
npm install
npm run dev     # rebuild dist/ on save
npm run build   # tsc --noEmit && vite build — must pass before you finish
```

## The manifest: `tab.json`

| Key | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| `schema_version` | `1` |
| `kind` | `"tab"` |
| `id` | lowercase letters, digits, inner hyphens; equals the folder name. Never change it. |
| `name` | 1–64 characters |
| `version` | semver, e.g. `0.1.0` |
| `description` | optional, ≤ 280 characters |
| `icon` | optional relative `.svg` path, ≤ 32 KiB, monochrome with `currentColor` |
| `api_version` | `1` |
| `entry` | `dist/index.html` |
| `tab.label` | the tab title, 1–24 characters (defaults to `name`) |
| `permissions` | scopes the tab needs (below); unknown scopes are errors |
| `network` | optional `{ "allowed_origins": ["https://host"], "reasoning": "why" }` |
| `configuration` | optional settings form (below) |

Unknown keys are errors. Paths are relative, without `..` or backslashes.

### Permissions (scopes)

Ask only for what the tab uses. Adding a scope or origin asks the user to
approve the tab again.

| Scope | Allows |
| --- | --- |
| `features:read` | the current project and feature, the project's features, feature events |
| `git:read` | status, diff, log and branches of the current feature; the `git.status` stream |
| `forge:read` | pull/merge request status of the feature; the `forge.status` stream |
| `files:read` | file tree and file contents of the feature's worktree; `files.changes` |
| `agent:observe` | the feature's agent session history; `session.stream` |
| `workspace:read` | every project and feature; `session.status` |

Always available without a scope: context, theme, `state`, `storage`,
`config`, `ui.*`.

### Network

To call an external service, list its origin (`https://host[:port]`, no path,
no wildcard, at most 16) in `network.allowed_origins` with a `reasoning`, then
use `client.net.fetch(...)`. Cadencr relays the request; the page still has no
direct network. `http://localhost` is accepted for local development only.

### Configuration

Each field: `{ "key", "type", "label", "scope" }`. `type` is `string`,
`number`, `boolean`, `select` (needs `options`) or `secret` (no `default`).
`scope` is `global` or `project`. Keys match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,47}$`. The user
fills the form in Settings; read values with `client.config.get()`. Secrets are
never readable by the tab; write `{{secret:<key>}}` in a `net.fetch` header and
Cadencr substitutes it.

## The SDK: `@cadencr/tab-sdk`

```tsx
import { CadencrProvider, useCadencr, useStream, useTabContext, useTheme } from "@cadencr/tab-sdk/react";
```

- `<CadencrProvider fallback={…}>` wraps the app (see `src/main.tsx`).
- `useTabContext()` → `{ project: { id, name, path }, feature: { id, name,
  branch, baseBranch, worktreePath }, pullRequest | null, session | null }`.
- `useTheme()` → `{ id, appearance: "light" | "dark", tokens }`.
- `useCadencr()` → the client:
  - `client.api.request({ method, path, query?, body? })` → `{ status,
    json<T>() }` — Cadencr's HTTP API, limited to your scopes and bound to
    the current feature (e.g. `GET /api/git/changed-files` with
    `query: { feature_id, mode: "uncommitted" }`).
  - `client.ui.toast({ title, description?, variant? })`.
  - `client.storage`, `client.state`, `client.config`, `client.net`.
- `useStream("git.status", (event) => …)` — live events; the payload is
  `event.data.payload`. Topics: `git.status`, `files.changes`, `forge.status`,
  `session.stream`, `feature.events`, `session.status` (each needs its scope).

Handle errors visibly (a toast or inline message); never swallow them.

## Design

- Build the UI from `@cadencr/tab-kit` components (`Button`, `Card`,
  `Badge`, `Input`, …) so the tab looks like the rest of Cadencr.
- Style with the kit's Tailwind tokens (`bg-background`, `text-foreground`,
  `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, …). Never hardcode colors: the
  user's theme — light or dark, any palette — is injected for you.
- Keep it calm and dense: small type, generous spacing, no decorative
  gradients or shadows.
